"── 94_winui_drawing.mst — WG4 D3: the custom-drawn command bar ──────────
 docs/win_gui_design.md §2.1's Toolbar row, which is a refusal before it is a
 design: *a custom-drawn command bar (one owner-drawn strip, NOT comctl's
 dated TOOLBARCLASS chrome)*. What replaces it:

   * view glyphs from **Segoe Fluent Icons** — the system icon font, Windows'
     own answer to SF Symbols — **with 9pt labels beneath** them. That is
     §2.5's deliberate divergence from the Mac (weakness #1: an icon with no
     label is a puzzle), and the Mac may adopt it back.
   * the active view marked by an **accent-colour underline** — the Windows
     tab idiom, the thing Settings and Edge do — with the accent **READ FROM
     THE SYSTEM**, never invented here.
   * Do It / Print It as labelled buttons on the same strip.

 THE ONE PLACE §2.4a BENDS, and it is stated in the sprint design before it
 was coded. Every other message FLAGS and returns, and the drain does the
 work. `WM_DRAWITEM` cannot: a paint deferred to the drain leaves the control
 blank until something else repaints it, which is not a latency problem but a
 wrong picture. So this handler DRAWS, inline, and the permission it operates
 under is narrow and checkable:

   * it draws from values ALREADY COPIED out of the `DRAWITEMSTRUCT` by the
     door (D3's payload-lifetime rule — the pointer dies with the call and is
     never stashed);
   * every GDI call goes into a DC Windows just handed us;
   * it creates no window, moves no window, sends no message, and calls no
     other VM entry — so it cannot provoke the re-entrancy the drain exists
     to prevent;
   * it allocates GDI objects and DELETES them in the same method, because a
     leak here is one per repaint and repaints are unbounded.

 If that permission ever proves too strong — if a paint handler is measured
 provoking a message — the fallback is the Cocoa answer, written down in the
 sprint doc so it is a decision rather than a scramble: draw into a cached
 bitmap in the drain, and let the handler blit it."

"── the GDI surface, and only what the bar needs ─────────────────────────
 Every one resolved by NAME through the P5 winkb resolver, exactly as WG0's
 user32 calls are. No new mechanism, and deliberately no more of GDI than the
 strip actually draws with."
Object subclass: WinApi [

    WinApi class >> createSolidBrush: colorref <Integer> ^ <Integer> [
        <primitive: FFI function: #CreateSolidBrush ret: #g args: #(g)>
        ^self primitiveFailed: 'CreateSolidBrush'
    ]

    WinApi class >> fillRect: hdc <Integer> rect: rectPtr <Integer> brush: hbr <Integer> ^ <Integer> [
        <primitive: FFI function: #FillRect ret: #g args: #(g g g)>
        ^self primitiveFailed: 'FillRect'
    ]

    WinApi class >> deleteObject: hgdiobj <Integer> ^ <Integer> [
        <primitive: FFI function: #DeleteObject ret: #g args: #(g)>
        ^self primitiveFailed: 'DeleteObject'
    ]

    WinApi class >> selectObject: hdc <Integer> object: hgdiobj <Integer> ^ <Integer> [
        <primitive: FFI function: #SelectObject ret: #g args: #(g g)>
        ^self primitiveFailed: 'SelectObject'
    ]

    WinApi class >> setTextColor: hdc <Integer> color: colorref <Integer> ^ <Integer> [
        <primitive: FFI function: #SetTextColor ret: #g args: #(g g)>
        ^self primitiveFailed: 'SetTextColor'
    ]

    WinApi class >> setBkMode: hdc <Integer> mode: m <Integer> ^ <Integer> [
        <primitive: FFI function: #SetBkMode ret: #g args: #(g g)>
        ^self primitiveFailed: 'SetBkMode'
    ]

    WinApi class >> drawText: hdc <Integer> text: textPtr <Integer> count: c <Integer>
                     rect: rectPtr <Integer> format: fmt <Integer> ^ <Integer> [
        <primitive: FFI function: #DrawTextW ret: #g args: #(g g g g g)>
        ^self primitiveFailed: 'DrawTextW'
    ]

    WinApi class >> createFontHeight: cHeight <Integer> width: cWidth <Integer>
                     escapement: cEsc <Integer> orientation: cOri <Integer>
                     weight: cWeight <Integer> italic: bItalic <Integer>
                     underline: bUnder <Integer> strikeOut: bStrike <Integer>
                     charSet: iCharSet <Integer> outPrecision: iOut <Integer>
                     clipPrecision: iClip <Integer> quality: iQuality <Integer>
                     pitchAndFamily: iPitch <Integer> face: facePtr <Integer> ^ <Integer> [
        "All fourteen, because the resolver requires the token list to match the
         selector's arity exactly — and that refusal is right: a 14-token pragma
         on a 3-argument method would have marshalled eleven words of whatever
         happened to be next on the stack."
        <primitive: FFI function: #CreateFontW ret: #g args: #(g g g g g g g g g g g g g g)>
        ^self primitiveFailed: 'CreateFontW'
    ]

    WinApi class >> createFont: height <Integer> weight: w <Integer> face: facePtr <Integer> ^ <Integer> [
        "The three arguments this bar actually varies. Everything else is the
         documented default, and 0 means 'the system decides' — the restraint
         principle, in an argument list. CLEARTYPE_QUALITY (5) is the one
         deliberate non-zero: text this small is unreadable without it."
        ^self
            createFontHeight: height width: 0
            escapement: 0 orientation: 0
            weight: w italic: 0
            underline: 0 strikeOut: 0
            charSet: 1 outPrecision: 0
            clipPrecision: 0 quality: 5
            pitchAndFamily: 0 face: facePtr
    ]
]

Object subclass: WinShell [

    "── the system accent, read and never invented ───────────────────────"

    WinShell class >> accentColorRef ^ <Integer> [
        "HKCU\\Software\\Microsoft\\Windows\\DWM\\AccentColor is already a
         COLORREF (0x00BBGGRR), which is what every GDI call here wants — so
         this is a read with no arithmetic on it at all.

         The fallback is Windows' own default accent blue rather than a colour
         chosen here: a preference this project made up would not be the
         host's, which is the same sentence `appsUseLightTheme` is written
         under."
        | v |
        AccentColor isNil ifFalse: [ ^AccentColor ].
        v := WinApi
            dwordAt: (WinApi constant: 'HKEY_CURRENT_USER')
            path: 'Software\Microsoft\Windows\DWM'
            value: 'AccentColor'.
        AccentColor := v isNil
            ifTrue: [ 16r00D77800 ]
            ifFalse: [ v bitAnd: 16r00FFFFFF ].
        ^AccentColor
    ]

    WinShell class >> barTextColorRef ^ <Integer> [
        "Monochrome chrome (§2.1's Colour row): near-black on light, near-white
         on dark. Accent is spent ONLY on the active-view underline and focus
         rings, which is what keeps it meaning something."
        ^self systemPrefersDark ifTrue: [ 16r00E8E8E8 ] ifFalse: [ 16r00202020 ]
    ]

    WinShell class >> barDimTextColorRef ^ <Integer> [
        ^self systemPrefersDark ifTrue: [ 16r00A0A0A0 ] ifFalse: [ 16r00707070 ]
    ]

    "── the two fonts the strip draws with ───────────────────────────────
     §2.1's Type row: Segoe UI Variable for text at Fluent's 9pt, and Segoe
     Fluent Icons for the glyphs. Both ship with Win11. Created ONCE and kept
     — a font per repaint would be a leak with a frame rate."

    WinShell class >> pointsToHeight: pt <Integer> ^ <Integer> [
        "CreateFontW's height is in logical units and NEGATIVE means 'character
         height, not cell height' — the form every Windows UI font is asked for.
         Scaled by the window's own DPI, so 9pt is 9pt on any monitor."
        ^0 - (pt * (Dpi isNil ifTrue: [ 96 ] ifFalse: [ Dpi ]) // 72)
    ]

    WinShell class >> labelFont ^ <Integer> [
        LabelFont isNil ifTrue: [
            LabelFont := WinApi
                createFont: (self pointsToHeight: 9)
                weight: 400
                face: (WinArena nativeUtf16: 'Segoe UI Variable Text') ].
        ^LabelFont
    ]

    WinShell class >> glyphFont ^ <Integer> [
        GlyphFont isNil ifTrue: [
            GlyphFont := WinApi
                createFont: (self pointsToHeight: 14)
                weight: 400
                face: (WinArena nativeUtf16: 'Segoe Fluent Icons') ].
        ^GlyphFont
    ]

    "── the glyphs, by view ──────────────────────────────────────────────
     Segoe Fluent Icons code points. Named here rather than inlined so the
     table reads as a table — and so a view that arrives without one gets a
     visible placeholder rather than a blank cell."

    WinShell class >> glyphCodeFor: aSymbol <Symbol> ^ <Integer> [
        "A Segoe Fluent Icons code point, as a NUMBER. Deliberately not a
         String: these live in the Private Use Area (U+E700..U+F8FF), and
         routing one through `String with: (Character value: ...)` and a UTF-16
         conversion is two chances to lose it — measured, and it lost it at the
         first, unwinding the paint handler with nothing drawn."
        aSymbol == #welcome    ifTrue: [ ^16rE80F ].  "Home"
        aSymbol == #transcript ifTrue: [ ^16rE9D5 ].  "Script"
        aSymbol == #metrics    ifTrue: [ ^16rE9D9 ].  "Diagnostic"
        aSymbol == #workspace  ifTrue: [ ^16rE70F ].  "Edit"
        aSymbol == #browser    ifTrue: [ ^16rE8A4 ].  "List"
        ^16rE897                                       "Help"
    ]

    WinShell class >> glyphBufferFor: aSymbol <Symbol> ^ <Integer> [
        "The glyph as a NUL-terminated UTF-16 string, written straight into the
         arena. One 32-bit store does the whole job — the code unit in the low
         half, the terminator in the high — which is what DrawTextW wants and
         skips every conversion between here and there."
        GlyphBuf isNil ifTrue: [ GlyphBuf := WinArena alloc: 8 ].
        WinArena u32At: GlyphBuf put: (self glyphCodeFor: aSymbol).
        ^GlyphBuf
    ]

    "── the handler the door calls ───────────────────────────────────────"

    WinShell class >> drawItem: anId <Integer> state: aState <Integer> dc: hdc <Integer>
                       x: x <Integer> y: y <Integer> w: w <Integer> h: h <Integer> ^ <Integer> [
        "WM_DRAWITEM, decoded by the door into seven SmallIntegers. Answers 1
         (handled) or 0 (let the default happen).

         Everything below is drawn from these VALUES. The `DRAWITEMSTRUCT`
         they came out of is already gone by the time this runs, which is the
         constraint D3 stated and the reason the door copies rather than
         passing the pointer through."
        | c |
        DrawCalls := (DrawCalls isNil ifTrue: [ 0 ] ifFalse: [ DrawCalls ]) + 1.
        "FIVE, not six: `Array class>>with:` stops at five arguments, and the
         six-argument send raised a DNU that unwound this handler before it
         drew anything — instrumentation breaking the thing it was measuring."
        DrawLast := Array with: anId with: x with: y with: w with: h.
        DrawIds isNil ifTrue: [ DrawIds := OrderedCollection new ].
        (DrawIds includes: anId) ifFalse: [ DrawIds add: anId ].
        c := self controlWithId: anId.
        c isNil ifTrue: [ DrawMisses := (DrawMisses isNil ifTrue: [ 0 ] ifFalse: [ DrawMisses ]) + 1. ^0 ].
        "A raise inside a PAINT is invisible by nature — the cell simply stays
         blank and nothing says why. Captured rather than propagated, because
         an unhandled error here would also unwind through the door."
        DrawResult := [ self drawBarItem: c state: aState dc: hdc x: x y: y w: w h: h ]
            on: Error
            do: [ :e | DrawError := e messageText. 0 ].
        DrawResultClass := DrawResult class name.
        ^DrawResult
    ]

    WinShell class >> drawCalls ^ <Integer> [ ^DrawCalls isNil ifTrue: [ 0 ] ifFalse: [ DrawCalls ] ]
    WinShell class >> drawLast ^ <Object> [ ^DrawLast ]
    WinShell class >> drawResult ^ <Object> [ ^DrawResult ]
    WinShell class >> drawStep ^ <Object> [ ^DrawStep ]
    WinShell class >> drawResultClass ^ <Object> [ ^DrawResultClass ]
    WinShell class >> drawIds ^ <Object> [ ^DrawIds isNil ifTrue: [ OrderedCollection new ] ifFalse: [ DrawIds ] ]
    WinShell class >> drawMisses ^ <Integer> [ ^DrawMisses isNil ifTrue: [ 0 ] ifFalse: [ DrawMisses ] ]
    WinShell class >> drawError ^ <String> [ ^DrawError isNil ifTrue: [ '' ] ifFalse: [ DrawError ] ]

    WinShell class >> drawBarItem: c <WinControl> state: aState <Integer> dc: hdc <Integer>
                       x: x <Integer> y: y <Integer> w: w <Integer> h: h <Integer> ^ <Integer> [
        | rect isActive isPressed glyph label brush old |
        DrawStep := 1.
        rect := self drawRectScratch.
        DrawStep := 2.
        isPressed := (aState bitAnd: (WinApi constant: 'ODS_SELECTED')) ~= 0.
        isActive := self activeViewButtonName == c name.

        "1. The cell's own background. A pressed cell gets a subtle wash; the
            active one gets nothing but its underline, because the underline IS
            the selection in this idiom."
        self setRect: rect x: x y: y w: w h: h.
        DrawStep := 3.
        brush := WinApi createSolidBrush: (isPressed
            ifTrue: [ self systemPrefersDark ifTrue: [ 16r003A3A3A ] ifFalse: [ 16r00E8E8E8 ] ]
            ifFalse: [ self backgroundColorRef ]).
        DrawStep := 4.
        WinApi fillRect: hdc rect: rect brush: brush.
        DrawStep := 5.
        WinApi deleteObject: brush.
        DrawStep := 6.
        WinApi setBkMode: hdc mode: (WinApi constant: 'TRANSPARENT').
        DrawStep := 7.

        "2. The glyph, then the label beneath it — the two-row cell §2.1 asks
            for, and the reason a view-bar button is 34 DIPs tall rather than a
            push button's 23."
        glyph := self glyphBufferFor: (self viewSymbolForButton: c name).
        DrawStep := 8.
        label := c barLabel.
        DrawStep := 9.
        (self isViewButton: c name)
            ifTrue: [
                WinApi setTextColor: hdc color: (isActive
                    ifTrue: [ self barTextColorRef ]
                    ifFalse: [ self barDimTextColorRef ]).
                DrawStep := 10.
                old := WinApi selectObject: hdc object: self glyphFont.
                DrawStep := 11.
                self setRect: rect x: x y: y + (2 * h // 100) w: w h: h * 55 // 100.
                DrawStep := 12.
                WinApi drawText: hdc
                    text: glyph count: 0 - 1
                    rect: rect
                    format: ((WinApi constant: 'DT_CENTER')
                        bitOr: ((WinApi constant: 'DT_BOTTOM') bitOr: (WinApi constant: 'DT_SINGLELINE'))).
                WinApi selectObject: hdc object: old.
                old := WinApi selectObject: hdc object: self labelFont.
                self setRect: rect x: x y: y + (h * 55 // 100) w: w h: h * 45 // 100.
                WinApi drawText: hdc
                    text: (WinArena scratchUtf16: label) count: 0 - 1
                    rect: rect
                    format: ((WinApi constant: 'DT_CENTER')
                        bitOr: ((WinApi constant: 'DT_TOP') bitOr: (WinApi constant: 'DT_SINGLELINE'))).
                WinApi selectObject: hdc object: old ]
            ifFalse: [
                "A verb button — Do It / Print It: one centred label, greyed when
                 its own control is disabled, which is D4's contract showing
                 through in the only place it can be seen."
                WinApi setTextColor: hdc color: (c isEnabled
                    ifTrue: [ self barTextColorRef ]
                    ifFalse: [ self barDimTextColorRef ]).
                old := WinApi selectObject: hdc object: self labelFont.
                self setRect: rect x: x y: y w: w h: h.
                WinApi drawText: hdc
                    text: (WinArena scratchUtf16: label) count: 0 - 1
                    rect: rect
                    format: ((WinApi constant: 'DT_CENTER')
                        bitOr: ((WinApi constant: 'DT_VCENTER') bitOr: (WinApi constant: 'DT_SINGLELINE'))).
                WinApi selectObject: hdc object: old ].

        "3. The accent underline, and ONLY under the active view. Three DIPs of
            the system's own accent colour: the whole of how a Windows app says
            'you are here' since Windows 10, and the reason this bar needs no
            selection frame, no sunken border and no second colour."
        (isActive and: [ self isViewButton: c name ]) ifTrue: [
            | uh |
            uh := 3 * (Dpi isNil ifTrue: [ 96 ] ifFalse: [ Dpi ]) // 96.
            self setRect: rect x: x + (w // 4) y: y + h - uh w: w // 2 h: uh.
            brush := WinApi createSolidBrush: self accentColorRef.
            WinApi fillRect: hdc rect: rect brush: brush.
            WinApi deleteObject: brush ].
        ^1
    ]

    "── the small helpers the handler leans on ───────────────────────────"

    WinShell class >> drawRectScratch ^ <Integer> [
        "One RECT, reused for every cell of every repaint. A fresh allocation
         per paint would be an allocation per frame, and the arena is exactly
         the tool that makes not doing that easy."
        DrawRect isNil ifTrue: [ DrawRect := WinArena alloc: (WinApi sizeOf: 'RECT') ].
        ^DrawRect
    ]

    WinShell class >> setRect: ptr <Integer> x: x <Integer> y: y <Integer> w: w <Integer> h: h <Integer> [
        WinArena u32At: ptr + (WinApi offsetOf: 'RECT' field: 'left') put: x.
        WinArena u32At: ptr + (WinApi offsetOf: 'RECT' field: 'top') put: y.
        WinArena u32At: ptr + (WinApi offsetOf: 'RECT' field: 'right') put: x + w.
        WinArena u32At: ptr + (WinApi offsetOf: 'RECT' field: 'bottom') put: y + h.
        ^self
    ]

    WinShell class >> isViewButton: aName <Symbol> ^ <Boolean> [
        ^self views anySatisfy: [ :v | (self viewButtonNameFor: (v at: 1)) == aName ]
    ]

    WinShell class >> viewSymbolForButton: aName <Symbol> ^ <Symbol> [
        self views do: [ :v |
            (self viewButtonNameFor: (v at: 1)) == aName ifTrue: [ ^v at: 1 ] ].
        ^aName
    ]

    WinShell class >> invalidateBar [
        "Ask Windows to repaint every owner-drawn cell, LATER. Called at the end
         of a layout pass and after a view switch — the two moments the strip's
         picture goes stale — and safe to call from inside the drain precisely
         because it queues rather than sends."
        self controls do: [ :c |
            (self isBarControl: c name) ifTrue: [
                "erase: 0 — the handler paints every pixel of the cell itself,
                 so erasing to the background first is a flash with no purpose."
                WinApi invalidateRect: c handle rect: 0 erase: 0 ] ].
        ^self
    ]

    WinShell class >> isBarControl: aName <Symbol> ^ <Boolean> [
        ^(self isViewButton: aName) or: [ aName == #doit or: [ aName == #printit ] ]
    ]

    WinShell class >> activeViewButtonName ^ <Object> [
        ActiveView isNil ifTrue: [ ^nil ].
        ^self viewButtonNameFor: ActiveView
    ]
]

"── WinControl: the label it draws with, and whether it is enabled ───────"
Object subclass: WinControl [
    barLabel ^ <String> [
        "The text the control was created with — asked of Win32 rather than
         remembered, so a label changed by anything else still draws right."
        ^self text
    ]

    isEnabled ^ <Boolean> [
        ^WinApi asBool: (WinApi isWindowEnabled: handle)
    ]
]

Object subclass: WinApi [
    WinApi class >> invalidateRect: hwnd <Integer> rect: rectPtr <Integer> erase: bErase <Integer> ^ <Integer> [
        "ASYNCHRONOUS, and that asymmetry with `SendMessageW` is the whole
         reason this call is the answer to WG4 D3's real problem.

         An owner-drawn control is painted by `WM_DRAWITEM` sent to its parent
         — and every moment the shell would naturally provoke one (creating the
         control, `SetWindowPos` from the layout) happens INSIDE a VM entry
         holding the busy guard, so the door correctly declines it and the cell
         stays blank. Measured exactly that way: `drawCalls` 0 with `busy` 16.

         `InvalidateRect` does not send anything. It marks the control dirty and
         RETURNS; Windows delivers the paint later, from the queue, through the
         pump — by which time the drain has released the guard and the VM is
         idle. So the paint arrives at a door that will let it in, with no
         exemption from the busy guard, no nested entry, and no re-entrancy for
         the depth guard to have to make safe."
        <primitive: FFI function: #InvalidateRect ret: #g args: #(g g g)>
        ^self primitiveFailed: 'InvalidateRect'
    ]

    WinApi class >> isWindowEnabled: hwnd <Integer> ^ <Integer> [
        <primitive: FFI function: #IsWindowEnabled ret: #g args: #(g)>
        ^self primitiveFailed: 'IsWindowEnabled'
    ]
]
